<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TActiveTableRow and TActiveTableCell</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveTableRow" Text="TActiveTableRow API Manual" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveTableCell" Text="TActiveTableCell API Manual" />
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1.9" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TActiveTableRow</tt> is the active control counterpart of <tt>TTableRow</tt>.
<tt>TActiveTableCell</tt> is the active control counterpart of <tt>TTableCell</tt>.
Both controls appear inside a <tt>TTable</tt> and accept the properties of their regular
counterparts, such as <tt>HorizontalAlign</tt>, <tt>VerticalAlign</tt> and <tt>CssClass</tt>.
Both controls add two abilities: they make the row or the cell clickable, and they replace
their own client-side content during a callback response.
</p>

<h2>Responding to a Click</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A cell becomes clickable when you attach a handler to its <tt>OnCellSelected</tt> event.
A row becomes clickable when you attach a handler to its <tt>OnRowSelected</tt> event.
A control without a handler renders as a plain table row or cell and registers no client script.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TTable&gt;
  &lt;com:TActiveTableRow ID="row1"&gt;
    &lt;com:TActiveTableCell ID="cell1" OnCellSelected="cellSelected"&gt;Click me&lt;/com:TActiveTableCell&gt;
  &lt;/com:TActiveTableRow&gt;
  &lt;com:TActiveTableRow ID="row2" OnRowSelected="rowSelected"&gt;
    &lt;com:TActiveTableCell ID="cell2"&gt;Click anywhere in this row&lt;/com:TActiveTableCell&gt;
  &lt;/com:TActiveTableRow&gt;
&lt;/com:TTable&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The handler receives an event parameter that carries the position of the clicked element.
</p>

<!-- tabular: align=|l|l|, width=(0.4 0.4) -->
<table class="tabular">
	<tr>
		<th>Event</th>
		<th>Event parameter property</th>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td><tt>OnCellSelected</tt></td>
		<td><tt>SelectedCellIndex</tt>, the zero-based index of the cell within its row</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td><tt>OnRowSelected</tt></td>
		<td><tt>SelectedRowIndex</tt>, the zero-based index of the row within its table</td>
	</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
A cell click also bubbles up to the containing row. When both handlers are present, PRADO raises
<tt>OnCellSelected</tt> first and then raises <tt>OnRowSelected</tt> on the parent row. Attaching
both handlers along the same click path also makes the row and the cell each issue their own
callback request, so <tt>OnRowSelected</tt> runs more than once for a single click. Keep the row
handler on rows whose cells carry no handler of their own.
</p>

<h2>Updating Content on Callback</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Both controls replace their client-side content the way <tt>TActivePanel</tt> does. Call
<tt>render</tt> with a new writer obtained from the event parameter.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function cellSelected($sender, $param)
{
	$sender->Text = 'Cell ' . $param->SelectedCellIndex . ' was clicked';
	$sender->render($param->NewWriter);
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
Rendering a row replaces every cell in that row. PRADO therefore disables the individual updates
of the active controls inside the rendered row, because the parent already carries their new content.
</p>

<h2>Placement Requirements</h2>

<!-- tabular: align=|l|l|, width=(0.4 0.4) -->
<table class="tabular">
	<tr>
		<th>Condition</th>
		<th>Result</th>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>The row has a <tt>TTable</tt> ancestor</td>
		<td><tt>RowIndex</tt> returns the position of the row</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>The row has no <tt>TTable</tt> ancestor</td>
		<td>PRADO throws a <tt>TConfigurationException</tt></td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>The cell has a <tt>TTableRow</tt> parent</td>
		<td><tt>CellIndex</tt> returns the position of the cell</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>The cell has no <tt>TTableRow</tt> parent</td>
		<td>PRADO throws a <tt>TConfigurationException</tt></td>
	</tr>
</table>

